From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 00:36:52 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: max17042_battery: Keep the SOC alert on RepSOC

The SOC alert compares SALRT_Th with the register MiscCFG.SACFG selects.
Since commit 3a3acf839b2c ("power: supply: max17042_battery: Fix SOC
threshold calc w/ no current sense") the driver takes the thresholds
from RepSOC when a sense resistor is described and from VFSOC when it
is not, but it sets SACFG only in the second case, to VFSOC.  With a
sense resistor it relies on SACFG still being at its power-on value.
The gauge keeps MiscCFG for as long as the battery is connected, so a
boot of the same driver without the resistor in the device tree, or the
firmware, can leave the alert on VFSOC while the thresholds follow
RepSOC.

That is what happened on a Samsung Galaxy S10+ (MAX77705) after its
device tree gained the sense resistor.  With the battery full, RepSOC
read 100.0 % and VFSOC 104.9 %, so the window became 99..101 %, SMX was
set again the moment the handler cleared it, and the handler wrote the
same window back.  Measured over 10 s on an idle system: 108 gauge
interrupts per second, 10154 per second on the I2C controller, upowerd
in uninterruptible sleep behind the power_supply_changed() storm, and
85 % idle CPU.  Setting SACFG to RepSOC by hand brought the gauge
interrupt to 0/s, the I2C controller to 14/s and idle CPU to 99 %.

Set SACFG to RepSOC whenever the driver reads RepSOC, before the
interrupt is requested.  On boards that were already consistent this
writes the value the register holds.  The voltage-only setup also zeroes
CGAIN, and that is left alone here: the gain is encoded differently on
the ModelGauge m5 parts, where 0x0400 is unity (the value that brings the
current reading back on this MAX77705), than on the MAX17047, where it is
0x4000.

Fixes: 3a3acf839b2c ("power: supply: max17042_battery: Fix SOC threshold calc w/ no current sense")
Signed-off-by: Artur Szafraniec <ganggg111@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/power/supply/max17042_battery.c | 17 +++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+)

diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/max17042_battery.c b/drivers/power/supply/max17042_battery.c
index d409d2f0d38..72af2076980 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/max17042_battery.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/max17042_battery.c
@@ -40,6 +40,10 @@
 #define CFG_ALRT_BIT_ENBL	(1 << 2)
 #define CFG2_DSOCI_BIT_ENBL	(1 << 7)
 
+/* MiscCFG: the SOC register the SOC alert compares against */
+#define MISCCFG_SACFG_MASK	0x3
+#define MISCCFG_SACFG_REPSOC	0x0
+
 #define VFSOC0_LOCK		0x0000
 #define VFSOC0_UNLOCK		0x0080
 #define MODEL_UNLOCK1	0X0059
@@ -1188,6 +1192,19 @@ static int max17042_probe(struct i2c_client *client, struct device *dev, int irq
 		regmap_write(chip->regmap, MAX17042_CGAIN, 0x0000);
 		regmap_write(chip->regmap, MAX17042_MiscCFG, 0x0003);
 		regmap_write(chip->regmap, MAX17042_LearnCFG, 0x0007);
+	} else {
+		/*
+		 * The SOC thresholds are taken from RepSOC in this mode, so
+		 * the alert has to compare against RepSOC too.  The gauge
+		 * keeps MiscCFG for as long as the battery is connected, and
+		 * the voltage-only setup above, from a boot that described no
+		 * sense resistor, or the firmware may have left the alert on
+		 * VFSOC.  Thresholds around one register and an alert on
+		 * another keep the alert asserted, and the interrupt fires
+		 * again as soon as it is acknowledged.
+		 */
+		regmap_update_bits(chip->regmap, MAX17042_MiscCFG,
+				   MISCCFG_SACFG_MASK, MISCCFG_SACFG_REPSOC);
 	}
 
 	chip->task_period = MAX17042_DEFAULT_TASK_PERIOD;
